\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Tree frames and curvature of a hopping weight}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{In rooted tree coordinates for pure gauge fields
(\ref{fdip-00MT}), transport from a chosen root gives the frame
at each vertex along a spanning tree. Tree links then become
identities and the remaining chord transports share conjugation at
the root. A [scalar frame](fdip-010X) is supplied by the scalar
at its own site. It keeps a reduced group element on every edge,
including every edge of any spanning tree. The remaining
transformations at the constant scalar take values in its
stabilizer independently at the vertices.}

\p{For a compact free unit orbit with positive real hopping
coefficient #{w_e}, let #{v(t)} be a smooth path of group elements
through the identity at #{t=0}. Put #{o(t)=v(t)\phi^\circ}.
Differentiating #{\|o(t)\|^2=1} gives
#{\langle\phi^\circ,o'(0)\rangle=0} and
#{\langle\phi^\circ,o''(0)\rangle=-\|o'(0)\|^2}, using the
real pairing. The reduced hopping factor
#{B_e(v(t))=\ue^{w_e\langle\phi^\circ,o(t)\rangle}} has zero
first derivative and second derivative
#{-w_e\ue^{w_e}\|o'(0)\|^2} at zero. This gives the local
quadratic decrease of a single edge weight. A spectral mass
would require a separate statement about correlations or the
transfer spectrum.}
